---
import Navigation from "../../components/navigation.astro";
import { posts } from "../../data/posts.js";

const post = posts.find((post) => post.slug === "post_three");

if (!post) {
  throw new Error("Blog post not found.");
}

const { title, date, tags } = post;
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <title>{title} | Ivy Duncan</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content={post.excerpt}
    />
  </head>

  <body>
    <Navigation />

    <main>
      <article class="blog-post">
        <div class="container narrow">
          <header class="post-header">
            <p class="eyebrow">From the Blog</p>

            <h1>{title}</h1>

            <p class="post-date">{date}</p>

            <div class="post-tags">
              {tags.map((tag) => (
                <a
                  href={`/blog/tag/${tag.toLowerCase().replace(/\s+/g, "-")}`}
                  class="post-tag"
                >
                  {tag}
                </a>
              ))}
            </div>
          </header>

          <div class="post-content">
  <p>
    Since professional editing for <em>Not Interested</em> will be kicking off soon,
    I thought I'd share some details on my editing process.
  </p>

  <p>
    When "writing a book"—well, correction, when "publishing" a book—the writing
    part is really only half the work.
  </p>

  <p>
    I started writing in middle school, probably around 2014. So I've been doing
    this for over ten years now. When I started out on Wattpad, it was great because
    I could just draft and write my books (I'll have another blog post talking about
    the process I follow for that at some point), and then I could post them and not
    worry about spelling errors (and there were a lot of them—I suck at spelling) and
    grammar.
  </p>

  <p>
    I wasn't professionally publishing my books or asking anyone for money to read
    them, so posting what I now think is shit writing online wasn't a huge deal.
  </p>

  <p>
    When I decided to start publishing my books, I began to learn how much extra work
    I would have to do.
  </p>

  <p>
    <em>Five Years and 2,000 Miles</em> was the first book I published. It had been
    on Wattpad for about two years before I decided I was going to edit it and publish
    it. And I quickly realized I am what I call an "over-writer."
  </p>

  <p>
    When I copied that into a single Word document, I learned that baby was over
    200k words.
  </p>

  <p>
    And so came the first step of my editing process.
  </p>

  <h2>Step One: Remove the Stuff That Isn't Needed</h2>
  <p class="step-subtitle">Reread #1</p>

  <p>
    This is really hard with romance, I think, because a lot of the things that
    aren't needed are cute conversations and sweet moments. But we don't need 200k
    of those.
  </p>

  <p>
    So, I reread my entire book, fix any spelling errors that stick out, highlight
    any sentences that read funny so I can re-evaluate them, and remove paragraphs
    of writing that aren't needed. I even take out entire chapters sometimes.
  </p>

  <h2>Step Two: What Is Missing?</h2>

  <p>
    A lot of times during my rereads, I notice that things are missing. Honestly,
    most of the time, since the books I've published so far have been college
    romances, I'm missing breaks.
  </p>

  <p>
    One of the last chapters I wrote in my upcoming book <em>Not Interested</em>
    was a spring break chapter because I got through my reread and realized, wait,
    this takes place during second semester. They have to have a spring break.
  </p>

  <p>
    I actually forgot about it in <em>If It's Meant to Be</em> as well, and had to
    rewrite the beginning of one of my chapters to make it so a week had passed and
    Hale had gone home and thought about his problems with Logan over spring break.
  </p>

  <p>
    These are the chapters and parts I add in this step.
  </p>

  <h2>Step Three: Go Back Over My Highlighted Sections and Fix Them</h2>

  <p>
    This is where I scroll through my file, find all the highlighted parts, and fix
    them.
  </p>

  <p>
    I also have a list of phrases and words I know I use too much saved on my phone,
    and I highlight every single instance of them throughout the book and go through
    and reword or remove them wherever I can.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you think my characters sigh a lot now, you'd be amazed by my first draft.
    It's like every single breath is a sigh.
  </p>

  <p>
    Lots of nodding too. And they're always doing way too much with their eyes and
    gazes. Ugh. I try to clean all of that up in this step.
  </p>

  <h2>Step Four: Reread #2</h2>

  <p>
    With all that stuff cleaned up, it's time to download my novel as a PDF document
    and open it in a PDF editor on my trusty iPad.
  </p>

  <p>
    I reread the whole thing again, and I mark it up with red as I go. Again,
    highlighting anything that reads weird and fixing any typos and things that
    stand out.
  </p>

  <p>
    Then, I go through and update the file on my laptop with these edits.
  </p>

  <h2>Step Five: Reread #3</h2>

  <p>
    I like to print my novels and read them in book form, so I do this. Usually by
    this point, it seems pretty clean to me, so I mark it up with a pen or pencil
    and flag those sections with sticky notes.
  </p>

  <p>
    Then, when I'm finished with the book, I make those changes on my laptop as well.
  </p>

  <h2>Step Six: Professional Editor!</h2>

  <p>
    I use Reedsy to find an editor and send the file over to them. It's really nice
    because I get a month-long break from my book while they're working on it.
  </p>

  <h2>Step Seven: Review the Editor's Notes</h2>

  <p>
    I look over all the suggested changes and make the ones I need to or want to.
    A lot are grammar-based and need to be fixed, but sometimes they suggest a
    certain wording or adding certain things to the story, and I take the ones I
    like and leave the ones I don't.
  </p>

  <h2>Step Eight: My Proofreader (Thanks, Mom!)</h2>

  <p>
    I hand my books over to my proofreader, who is my mother. She reads, gives me
    feedback, and texts me about anything off she notices, and I make small fixes
    according to what she finds.
  </p>

  <p class="post-ending">
    And then... I upload the file to Amazon and publish!
  </p>

  <p>
    The whole process really is a headache, and it takes <strong>months</strong>,
    but I wouldn't do it if I didn't love it.
  </p>
</div>


          <footer class="post-footer">
            <a href="/blog" class="back-link">← Back to the blog</a>
          </footer>
        </div>
      </article>
    </main>

    <footer class="site-footer">
      <div class="container">
        <p>© {new Date().getFullYear()} Ivy Duncan. All rights reserved.</p>
      </div>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>

<style>
  .container {
    width: min(1100px, calc(100% - 3rem));
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  .narrow {
    max-width: 800px;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  .blog-post {
    padding: 5rem 0;
  }

  .post-header {
    text-align: center;
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    color: #765f48;
    font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .post-header h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);
    line-height: 1.05;
  }

  .post-date {
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    color: #6f6a63;
    font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .post-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
  }

  .post-tag {
    color: #765f48;
    font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    text-decoration: none;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .post-tag:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }

  .post-content {
    color: #3f3a35;
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  .post-content p {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
  }

  .post-content strong {
    color: #765f48;
  }

  .post-footer {
    margin-top: 4rem;
    padding-top: 2rem;
    border-top: 1px solid #ddd6cd;
  }

  .back-link {
    color: #765f48;
    font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .back-link:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }

  .site-footer {
    padding: 2rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid #ddd6cd;
    color: #6f6a63;
    font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .container {
      width: min(100% - 2rem, 1100px);
    }

    .blog-post {
      padding: 3.5rem 0;
    }
  }
</style>